s1["t"] = "Start with what is in your pocket and what is in your account, because they are different things. The Bank of England, the central bank of the United Kingdom, set this out for the public in 2014. It calls each kind of money an IOU, which is a written promise to pay. Notes and coins are a promise from the central bank. The balance in your account is a promise from your bank to you. The Bank then gives the proportions: 97 per cent of the money held by the public is bank deposits, and only about 3 per cent is notes and coins. So when your pay arrives, nothing is placed in a box with your name on it. A number goes up in a private company's records. The company owes you that amount and is free to use the money in the meantime. It lends it to other people at a higher rate than it pays you, and the difference is its income. You are the lender in this arrangement, though no one describes it to you that way."
